    faq=[
        ("What is ROI in project management?", "Return on investment is the net benefit of a project (total benefits minus total costs) divided by its total cost and expressed as a percentage over a stated period. It is estimated in the business case and measured again after delivery."),
        ("What is the ROI formula?", "ROI = (total benefits − total costs) ÷ total costs × 100. For example, $230,000 of benefits against $195,000 of costs gives ($35,000 ÷ $195,000) × 100 = 17.9%."),
        ("What is a good ROI for a project?", "It depends on your organisation's hurdle rate, the alternatives and the risk of the benefits. Compare annualised ROI or IRR with the hurdle rate set by finance, and look at NPV for the total value created."),
        ("Can ROI be negative?", "Yes. A negative ROI means the project costs more than it returns over the period measured. Mandatory projects, such as compliance work, sometimes go ahead with a negative financial ROI; in that case use ROI to compare ways of meeting the requirement."),
        ("What is the difference between ROI and NPV?", "ROI is a percentage that, in its simple form, ignores when money arrives. NPV is a money value that discounts each year's cash flow to today's money. NPV is the better measure of value; ROI is easier to explain."),
        ("What is annualised ROI?", "The equivalent yearly rate of a multi-year ROI: (1 + ROI)^(1 ÷ years) − 1. A three-year ROI of 17.9% is about 5.7% a year. Use it to compare projects that run for different periods."),
        ("How do you measure ROI for internal projects?", "Value the cash savings, avoided costs and any revenue effect against a do-nothing baseline. Treat time savings as soft benefits unless the time is redeployed or the cost removed, and agree how each benefit will be measured after delivery."),
        ("Should internal staff time be included in ROI?", "Yes. Staff time has a cost even though it is not a new payment. Use the loaded hourly rate your finance team provides, so that internal and external costs are treated consistently."),
        ("How do you include intangible benefits in ROI?", "Usually you do not put them in the calculation. Describe them alongside the ROI, explain how they will be measured (for example, customer satisfaction scores) and only convert them to money where there is a credible, agreed method."),
        ("When should ROI be measured?", "Estimate it in the business case, refresh it at major decision points during the project, and measure actual ROI after delivery once benefits have had time to appear, often over one to three years."),
        ("Is ROI covered in the PMP exam?", "Project selection measures such as ROI, NPV, IRR, payback and benefit-cost ratio commonly appear in PMP and CAPM preparation materials, usually at a conceptual level. Check PMI's current exam content outline for the exact scope; our [PMP certification guide](/project-management/pmp-certification/) summarises the exam."),
    ],
